<ol class="breathe-horizontal" start="1"> 


  <li class="arxiv-result">
    <div class="is-marginless">
      <p class="list-title is-inline-block"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23290">arXiv:2610.23290</a>
        <span>&nbsp;[<a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.23290">pdf</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/ps/2610.23290">ps</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/format/2610.23290">other</a>]&nbsp;</span>
      </p>
      <div class="tags is-inline-block">
        <span class="tag is-small is-link tooltip is-tooltip-top" data-tooltip="Artificial Intelligence">cs.AI</span>
        </div>
      
    </div>
    
    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        CoTA++: Understanding the Repeat Curse in dMLLMs from an <span class="search-hit mathjax">Information</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">Flow</span> Perspective
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Zhao%2C+Q">Qiyan Zhao</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Zhang%2C+X">Xiaofeng Zhang</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Zhang%2C+X">Xu-Yao Zhang</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Han%2C+S">Shijie Han</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Li%2C+Z">Zhepeng Li</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Zhu%2C+S">Shunzhi Zhu</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Wang%2C+D">Da-Han Wang</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.23290v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        Diffusion-based multimodal large <span class="search-hit mathjax">language</span> models (dMLLMs) decode by iterative denoising with bidirectional&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.23290v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2610.23290v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.23290v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Diffusion-based multimodal large <span class="search-hit mathjax">language</span> models (dMLLMs) decode by iterative denoising with bidirectional <span class="search-hit mathjax">attention</span>, which precludes the standard autoregressive key--value cache. Practical deployments therefore rely on approximate caching that reuses intermediate states across denoising steps. Such caching, however, often makes a response repeat one token at adjacent positions in a run that breaks the sentence, a failure we term the \emph{Repeat Curse}. Tracing the context-token <span class="search-hit mathjax">information</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">flow</span> reveals three disruptions. Within a step, caching disperses the <span class="search-hit mathjax">attention</span> that anchors a position on nearby context tokens. Across steps, similarity-based refresh is not staleness-aware, so some positions are decoded against stale key--value states. Across layers, the deep-layer entropy of the context fails to converge where repetition arises. We accordingly propose CoTA++, a training-free, plug-and-play framework that counteracts each disruption where it originates: Context-Token <span class="search-hit mathjax">Attention</span> Re-anchoring re-forms the <span class="search-hit mathjax">attention</span> routing against the current context, Decode-Aware Refresh reserves part of the cache&#39;s refresh budget for the positions about to be committed, and Context-Token Entropy-Guided Voting discounts the confidence of a position by the deep-layer entropy of its context, which reorders the unmasking. The three components act at non-overlapping stages of a decoding step and compose freely with existing dMLLMs and caches. Across three dMLLMs and two caching backends, CoTA++ reduces the sample repetition rate of long responses from up to 99.6\% to at most 17.0\%, recovers most of the accuracy lost to caching on nine benchmarks, and keeps the speedup of caching at under 15\% overhead, making approximate caching reliable for dMLLMs.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.23290v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2610.23290v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 16 October, 2026; 
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> October 2026.
      
    </p>
    

    

    
  </li>

  <li class="arxiv-result">
    <div class="is-marginless">
      <p class="list-title is-inline-block"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17903">arXiv:2610.17903</a>
        <span>&nbsp;[<a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.17903">pdf</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/ps/2610.17903">ps</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/format/2610.17903">other</a>]&nbsp;</span>
      </p>
      <div class="tags is-inline-block">
        <span class="tag is-small is-link tooltip is-tooltip-top" data-tooltip="Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition">cs.CV</span>
        
          
            <span class="tag is-small is-grey tooltip is-tooltip-top" data-tooltip="Artificial Intelligence">cs.AI</span>
          
            <span class="tag is-small is-grey tooltip is-tooltip-top" data-tooltip="Robotics">cs.RO</span>
          
        </div>
      
    </div>
    
    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        DriveWLA: Unifying Future Dynamics and Scene Semantics through World-<span class="search-hit mathjax">Language</span>-Action Modeling for Autonomous Driving
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Wang%2C+S">Shuzheng Wang</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Li%2C+H">Haoxuan Li</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Li%2C+S">Shengzhen Li</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Wu%2C+C">Chenxi Wu</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Sun%2C+Y">Yajing Sun</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Hou%2C+L">Lu Hou</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Liu%2C+W">Wenyu Liu</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Wang%2C+X">Xinggang Wang</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.17903v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        Vision-<span class="search-hit mathjax">Language</span> Models (VLMs) and world models have demonstrated strong capabilities in semantic understanding and future scene evolution for autonomous driving. However, effectively integrating both capabilities for trajectory generation remains challenging. In this work, we propose DriveWLA, a unified World-&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.17903v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2610.17903v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.17903v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Vision-<span class="search-hit mathjax">Language</span> Models (VLMs) and world models have demonstrated strong capabilities in semantic understanding and future scene evolution for autonomous driving. However, effectively integrating both capabilities for trajectory generation remains challenging. In this work, we propose DriveWLA, a unified World-<span class="search-hit mathjax">Language</span>-Action model based on Mixture-of-Transformers (MoT) that jointly models semantic understanding, future scene dynamics, and trajectory generation. DriveWLA consists of three experts: a World Expert for future prediction, a <span class="search-hit mathjax">Language</span> Expert for semantic understanding, and an Action Expert for trajectory generation. We further introduce Cross-Expert <span class="search-hit mathjax">Attention</span>, which enables controlled <span class="search-hit mathjax">information</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">flow</span> across experts while aligning semantic and dynamic representations. To improve trajectory generation beyond imitation learning, we apply Flow-GRPO reinforcement learning post-training to refine the Action Expert. Extensive experiments show that DriveWLA achieves superior performance in both open-loop and closed-loop evaluation. DriveWLA achieves 92.3 PDMS on NAVSIM v1 and 90.1 EPDMS on NAVSIM v2, demonstrating strong performance among VLM-based and world-model-based planners. Without specific fine-tuning, DriveWLA further demonstrates strong zero-shot generalization to HUGSIM, nuScenes, and Bench2Drive. These results validate the effectiveness of our unified World-<span class="search-hit mathjax">Language</span>-Action modeling, establishing DriveWLA as a generalizable framework for autonomous driving.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.17903v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2610.17903v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 13 October, 2026; 
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> October 2026.
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="comments is-size-7">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Comments:</span>
      <span class="has-text-grey-dark mathjax">17 pages, 4 figures</span>
    </p>
    

    

    
  </li>

  <li class="arxiv-result">
    <div class="is-marginless">
      <p class="list-title is-inline-block"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.16349">arXiv:2610.16349</a>
        <span>&nbsp;[<a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.16349">pdf</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/ps/2610.16349">ps</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/format/2610.16349">other</a>]&nbsp;</span>
      </p>
      <div class="tags is-inline-block">
        <span class="tag is-small is-link tooltip is-tooltip-top" data-tooltip="Computation and Language">cs.CL</span>
        
          
            <span class="tag is-small is-grey tooltip is-tooltip-top" data-tooltip="Machine Learning">cs.LG</span>
          
        </div>
      
    </div>
    
    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        RIFT: Token Influence Follows the Path of Least Resistance
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Jin%2C+T">Tingan Jin</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Dong%2C+S">Shuhang Dong</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Li%2C+H">Haosong Li</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.16349v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        Which context tokens drive a <span class="search-hit mathjax">language</span> model&#39;s answer? Graph-based tools such as&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.16349v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2610.16349v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.16349v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Which context tokens drive a <span class="search-hit mathjax">language</span> model&#39;s answer? Graph-based tools such as <span class="search-hit mathjax">attention</span> rollout and <span class="search-hit mathjax">attention</span> flow answer this from a single forward pass, yet their faithfulness in decoder-only models is rarely tested against interventions. We view the layers of a decoder as a causal network whose conductances are <span class="search-hit mathjax">attention</span> weights, and read a token&#39;s influence on the answer from the resistance between them. We propose RIFT (Resistance-based <span class="search-hit mathjax">Information</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">Flow</span> in Transformers), which scores a token by the series resistance of its path of least resistance to the answer, found exactly by a classic shortest-path dynamic program. On synthetic in-context retrieval tasks and extractive question answering over Wikipedia paragraphs, RIFT predicts the effect of replacing a token with a Spearman correlation of 0.52-0.68; predicting a single replacement, it reaches 77-96% of the agreement between two different random replacements. It needs no gradients and no extra forward passes, scoring every token of a prompt in 0.9-3.4 ms on one CPU thread, whereas the replacement experiment needs a forward pass per token. On 576 held-out prompts from four models of two families (Qwen3 and Llama 3.2), it outperforms token distance, <span class="search-hit mathjax">attention</span> rollout and <span class="search-hit mathjax">attention</span> flow in every pre-specified comparison, by 0.23 to 0.88 in mean Spearman correlation, and it is the best or tied-best of all <span class="search-hit mathjax">attention</span>-based scores we test. Aggregates that add up all routes (<span class="search-hit mathjax">attention</span> flow, effective resistance and rollout) instead mostly encode token position: as a simple proposition shows, early tokens have many more parallel routes to the answer.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.16349v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2610.16349v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 12 October, 2026; 
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> October 2026.
      
    </p>
    
