    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        What the Model Cannot See: Covert and Provable Text Invisibility in LLMs
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Russinovich%2C+M">Mark Russinovich</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.15883v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        Text wrapped in a trigger can be made behaviorally invisible to a <span class="search-hit mathjax">language</span> model: omitted from summaries and quotations, ignored in question answering, and left unflagged by downstream filtering. We study this selective blindness as a dual-use&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.15883v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2610.15883v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.15883v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Text wrapped in a trigger can be made behaviorally invisible to a <span class="search-hit mathjax">language</span> model: omitted from summaries and quotations, ignored in question answering, and left unflagged by downstream filtering. We study this selective blindness as a dual-use <span class="search-hit mathjax">information</span>-<span class="search-hit mathjax">flow</span> primitive, a supply-chain backdoor, and a white-box auditing problem. We first implement it exactly as a serving-time span veto: blocking <span class="search-hit mathjax">information</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">flow</span> out of a marked span makes every outside activation bit-identical under same-length substitution of the span&#39;s content, a noninterference property we verify across ten models, seven mixer families, and 0.5B-120B parameters. The veto serves as a deployable quarantine, an exact reference for calibrating leakage, and a teacher. Distilled into weights, it yields a backdoor: checkpoints trained on six model families from 4B to 120B ignore marked context at little benchmark cost, and their outputs show no measurable semantic trace of the suppressed content above the veto reference on our held-out suite; an in-domain variant suppresses planted vulnerabilities during AI code review. Detection is therefore a white-box problem. Simpler backdoors remain internally transparent -- linear probes recover suppressed content at 0.57-0.79 accuracy versus 0.025 chance -- but hardening the attack until probes reach chance collapses <span class="search-hit mathjax">attention</span> from outside the span into it below the range of base models. Across the attacks we evaluate, probe visibility trades off against routing visibility; we do not establish that this tradeoff is fundamental.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.15883v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2610.15883v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 11 October, 2026; 
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> October 2026.
      
    </p>
    

    

    
